Memory Management

Memory management means how a programming language gives memory to a program and removes it when it is no longer needed.

There are mainly three approaches:
  
Approach             	    How it works	                                                                            Examples
Garbage Collector           Language automatically allocates and frees unused memory.	                                Java, JavaScript, Python
Manual Memory Management	Programmer manually allocates and deallocates memory.	                                    C, C++
Rust’s Ownership Model	    Rust manages memory automatically using ownership rules, without a garbage collector.       Rust



1. Garbage Collector

In languages like JavaScript and Java, memory is handled automatically by a Garbage Collector (GC).

The programmer creates variables and objects normally.
When an object is no longer being used or referenced, the garbage collector identifies it.
It automatically frees that unused memory.
This reduces problems such as dangling pointers and many memory leaks.
But the programmer has less direct control over when memory is freed.

Example languages: Java, JavaScript, Python

let user = { name: "Priyanshu" };

user = null; 
// The object may later be removed from memory by the Garbage Collector.


2. Manual Memory Management

In manual memory management, the programmer is responsible for allocating and freeing memory.

You request memory when needed.
You must release it after the work is finished.
If you forget to release memory, it can cause a memory leak.
If you access memory after freeing it, it can cause a dangling pointer error.
It gives more control and can be efficient, but it is harder to write safely.

Example language: C, cpp

#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *numbers = malloc(5 * sizeof(int)); // Allocate memory

    free(numbers); // Free memory after use
    return 0;
}
// If free(numbers) is not called, the allocated memory may remain occupied even after it is no longer useful.

3. The Rust Way: Ownership Model

Rust uses its own memory management system called the ownership model.

Rust does not use a garbage collector.
Rust also does not require you to manually call free() like C.
Every value has one owner.
When the owner goes out of scope, Rust automatically frees the memory.
Rust checks ownership and borrowing rules at compile time.
This makes Rust highly safe against common memory errors.

fn main() {
    {
        let name = String::from("Priyanshu");
        println!("{}", name);
    } // `name` goes out of scope here, so Rust frees its memory automatically.
}

Rust prevents many common problems before the program even runs:

Memory leaks in normal code
Dangling pointers
Double free errors
Data races in multithreaded programs

Simple Comparison

| Feature                   | Garbage Collector         | Manual Management     | Rust Ownership     |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------- | --------------------- | ------------------ |
| Who frees memory?         | Garbage Collector         | Programmer            | Rust automatically |
| Garbage Collector needed? | Yes                       | No                    | No                 |
| Manual `free()` needed?   | No                        | Yes                   | No                 |
| Memory safety             | Good                      | Depends on programmer | Very high          |
| Performance overhead      | Can have runtime overhead | Low                   | Low                |
| Examples                  | Java, JavaScript, Python  | C, C++                | Rust               |


Final Conclusion

JavaScript automatically manages memory using a garbage collector. 
C gives full memory control to the programmer but can cause memory-related bugs if handled incorrectly. 
Rust provides a middle path: it gives performance similar to C/C++ while using ownership rules to make memory management safe without a garbage collector.